[Event "World Championship Match-16th game"]
[Site "Moscow"]
[Date "1985.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "A. Karpov"]
[Black "G. Kasparov"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Link "https://www.chess.com/article/view/the-octopus-knight"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nb5 d6 6. c4 Nf6 7. N1c3 a6 8. Na3
d5 $6 {The mentioned gambit.} 9. cxd5 exd5 10. exd5 Nb4 11. Be2 $1 {Karpov's new
move, but Kasparov had a suprise waiting. 11 Bc4 Bg4 $1 had lead to a short draw
in the 12th game of the match.} 11... Bc5 $6 {A fantastic idea lies behind this
move, as well as a tactical flaw. Better was 11...Nbxd5 12 0-0 Be7 13 Nxd5 Nxd5
14 Bf3 Be6 15 Nc2.} 12. O-O $6 {12 Be3 $1 Bxe3 13 Qa4+ refutes Black's idea, and
retains the extra pawn without falling into a bind.} 12... O-O 13. Bf3 {After 13
Bg5 Nbxd5 14 Nxd5 Qxd5 15 Bxf6 Qxd1 16 Rxfd1 gxf6, black should drasw easily.}
13... Bf5 $1 {Preventing the knight from returning via c2 and securing d3 for his
own knight.} 14. Bg5 {14 Be3 Bxe3 15 fxe3 Qb6 was an alternative, and leads to
an unclear position.} 14... Re8 $1 {Black's plan unfolds smoothly, as he now
secures the e4-square before playing b5.} 15. Qd2 {White should be returning the
pawn by now, like with 15 Nc4 $5. Then 15...Bd3 16 a3 Bxc4 17 axb4 Bxb4 18 Re1
Rxe1+ 19 Qxe1 with complicated play.} 15... b5 $1 {The knight is now denied c4,
and the pawn fork b4 is now a threat.} 16. Rad1 {Alternatives were 16 Qf4 Bg6 17
Bxf6 Qxf6 18 Qxf6 gxf6 or 16 d6 Ra7 17 Rad1.} 16... Nd3 $1 17. Nab1 $2 {Karpov
drifts, not noticing what his position will soon be like. Better was 17 d6,
although the exchange sacrifice 17...Qxd6 $1 18 Bxa8 Rxa8 is good for black.}
17... h6 $1 {A good time to nudge the bishop, as it cannot drop back to e3.} 18.
Bh4 {18 Be3 $6 gives black a choice between 18...Rxe3 $5 19 fxe3 Qb6 or 18...Bxe3
19 fxe3 Qb6 20 Be2 Rxe3 21 Kh1 Bg6.} 18... b4 $1 {Now that h6 has been gotten in,
this exiles the knight to a4.} 19. Na4 {Or 19 Ne2 g5 $1 20 Bxg5 Nxf2 21 Rxf2 Bxf2+
22 Kxf2 hxg5 23 Qxg5+ Bg6 24 Nd2, after which black must play energetically to
exploit white's position.} 19... Bd6 20. Bg3 {20 Qc2 $2 loses to 20...Rc8 21 Qb3
Nf4 22 Rc1 Rxc1 23 Rxc1 g5 $1 24 Bg3 g4.} 20... Rc8 $1 {20...Ne4 $2 allows 21 Bxe4
Bxe4 22 Qe3, when white has no problems.} 21. b3 {Now 22 Nb2 is intended, after
which white will slowly unravel his pieces.} 21... g5 $3 {The first sign that
things have gone badly for Karpov. White's pieces will soon have no mobility.}
22. Bxd6 {22 Nb2 $2 loses to 22...Nxb2 23 Qxb2 g4 24 Be2 Rc2 winning the bishop.}
22... Qxd6 23. g3 {Now again black is faced with the threat of Nb2.} 23... Nd7 $3
24. Bg2 {24 Nb2 Qf6 $3 is good for black: 25 Nxd3 Bxd3 26 Bg4 Ne5 $1 gives him a
large advantage.} 24... Qf6 $1 {Black's masterplan has reduced white to a state of
helplessness.} 25. a3 a5 26. axb4 axb4 27. Qa2 {Only to bring a knight to d2
(where it does nothing), this move shows white's helplessness.} 27... Bg6 $1
{Opening a line to f2 and preventing white's active ideas.} 28. d6 {If white
does nothing, then black will have no problem finding a way to make progress. 28
Nd2 Re2 and f2 collapeses.} 28... g4 $1 {Black is not interested in the pawn
(28...Qxd6 $2 29 Nf2) and nails down the light squares near white's king instead.}
29. Qd2 {Back again, mission unaccomplished.} 29... Kg7 {There is no rush, white
can do nothing constructive.} 30. f3 {Short of time, Karpov breaks out to see
what is in store for him.} 30... Qxd6 {This frees the d7-knight for more active
service.} 31. fxg4 {31 Qb2+ is more tenacious, but it would not have changed
anything.} 31... Qd4+ 32. Kh1 Nf6 $1 {Threatening 33...Ne4 or 33...Nxg4.} 33. Rf4
{33 h3 gives black a choice between 33...Re3 or 33...Ne4.} 33... Ne4 $1 34. Qxd3
{The knight survived for 18 moves on d3, and played a major role in destroying
white's position.} 34... Nf2+ 35. Rxf2 {35 Kg1 Nh3++ 36 Kh1 Qxd3 37 Rxd3 Re1+ 38
Bf1 Nxf4 also loses for white.} 35... Bxd3 36. Rfd2 Qe3 $1 37. Rxd3 Rc1 $1 38. Nb2
Qf2 $1 39. Nd2 Rxd1+ {39...Re2 $1 actually mates next move.} 40. Nxd1 Re1+ {White
resigns.} 0-1